[size=3]1.9.1[/size]

[b]A game patch no longer takes the whole tool down.[/b] The tool finds the game's own ship code
by searching NMS.exe for byte patterns, and a patch that recompiles one function changes those
bytes. Until now a single changed instruction meant "0 signature matches (game code changed)" and
nothing worked. It now falls back to the closest match when only a few bytes differ, and still
refuses outright when two candidates are equally close, because a wrong address does not fail
loudly, it quietly runs the wrong code. If you hit that error on 1.9.0, this is the update for it.

[b]The ship generator is found without reading its own code at all.[/b] It is the function that
matters most and the one most likely to be recompiled, so it has a second way of being found: the
single place in the game that calls it, matched by the shape of the call rather than by its bytes.
A prologue rewritten past all recognition leaves that shape standing.

[b]Save > Game data says when a match was loose.[/b] The game-code check now reports which
functions were not matched the usual way and how, so a generated ship can be checked against the
game instead of trusted blindly. The failure message also carries the exe's path now, since a
second install, a leftover folder and a modded exe look identical without it.

[b]Ships you have in storage are listed.[/b] The freighter's storage bays are read like any other
slot, under the name each ship was stored as, with empty bays skipped.

[b]Landed and flying, for every ship that lands.[/b] The Fly/Land toggle used to be the Sentinel
interceptor's alone. It now covers fighters, explorers, shuttles, haulers, living ships and solar
sails, each from the game's own deploy animation. Freighters do not get it, because the game ships
no landing animation for one. They never set down.

[b]The solar sail was being drawn flying all along.[/b] Its scene is the only one the game models
in flight, mast up and sails out, which is why it alone hung three and a half units below the
ground when parked. Landed now puts its hull on the ground and flying opens the sails.

[b]A folding part is no longer swung about the wrong hinge.[/b] An animation's turn belongs to
that joint's own pivot, and some ships carry a second set of names for the same legs. Laying the
turn on the wrong copy put a hauler's rear leg two and a half units underground.

[b]Corvette modules draw the right part, and only one of it.[/b] A handed module drew both its
left and right halves at once. Mirroring one now swaps it for its opposite instead of leaving a
part inside out, and a leaning structural part is handed like any other.

[b]The gun hangs off its own mounting plate.[/b] Its five pieces, the plate, two arms, turret and
barrel, were not all hanging where the game puts them.

[b]Search odds are measured, not guessed.[/b] The part count is taken before the colour filter,
and the odds come from a measured sample, which catches cases the weights alone cannot.

[b]Backups clean up after themselves.[/b] Every change still backs your save up first, but the
oldest are now deleted as new ones arrive. Save > Backups sets how many to keep, twenty by
default, or zero to keep every one, and says what it deleted. Only the tool's own timestamped
copies are ever touched.

[b]The app is quicker and quieter.[/b] Ship pictures download several at a time instead of one
after another, so a save full of ships fills in about a second rather than ten. A ship already
drawn is not fetched or rebuilt again, and the 3D views stop redrawing when nothing is moving or
they are on a page you are not looking at.
